  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251995/assets/9905448ad59cb2f68f5def832811eb34056305d1a535f23387f4403a340cb8f8.png)\n\nIn $△OFH$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OF=\\frac{1}{2}|f|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OF}$ has the opposite direction to $f$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OF}=-\\frac{1}{2}f.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $FH=2|g|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{FH}$ has the opposite direction to $g$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{FH}=-2g.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\overset{\\to }{OF}+\\overset{\\to }{FH} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}f-2g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $h=\\lambda f+\\mu g=-\\frac{1}{2}f-2g.$\n\nSince $f∦g,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\mu =-2$",
